Fundamental constructs:
Git is a content-addressable filesystem. It means that at the core of Git is a simple key-value data store. 
You can insert any kind of content into a Git repository, for which Git will hand you back a unique key you can use later to retrieve that content.

You have objects and references
Objects split into
- blobs (file contents)
- trees (hierarchies of other trees with blobs as leafs (aka like UNIX directories))
- commits

For a detailled treatment how to work with the underlying objects refer to https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects 
One can inspect the underlying objects via their SHA-1 by `git cat-file -p <hash>`.
As a short reminder the order of the underlying operations for a typical Git workflow is as follows
- `git hash-object <file>`
- `git update-index --add --cacheinfo <hash>`
- `git update-index --add new.txt`
- `git write-tree` (stores all previous index additions into one tree)
- `git commit-tree <hash-of-tree> -p <parent-commit>` (creates an actual commit and paste it after parent)
Note that a commit can also have patch-ids, which are specific parts of another commit (hashes in a tree), which comes in handy
when their are conflicts due to pulling forced history rewrites from a remote origin or if one wants to partially reset changes.

There are special flavors of references (branches (HEAD -> master), tags, remotes and more).
Of particular interest is the HEAD reference which give the latest commit, which points to the previous history 
and will be the parent of the next commit (given by index) 


When Git initializes a directory it creates the following structure in `.git`

```
$ ls -F1
config
description
HEAD
hooks/
info/
objects/
refs/
```

The interesting parts are 

- config file contains your project-specific configuration options
- info directory keeps a global exclude file for ignored patterns that you don’t want to track in a .gitignore file
- The hooks directory contains your client- or server-side hook scripts
- the HEAD and (yet to be created) index files, and the objects and refs

You can fairly easily create your own tree. 
Git normally creates a tree by taking the state of your staging area or index and writing a series of tree objects from it.


Git References
```
$ find .git/refs
.git/refs
.git/refs/heads
.git/refs/tags
```
Instead of writing commits directly in a specific `.git/refs/<ref_name>` git uses internally something like this
```git update-ref refs/heads/master 1a410efbd13591db07496601ebc7a059dd55cfe9```
Changing the HEAD to another location works like this
`git symbolic-ref HEAD <ref-to-change-head-to>
Of course checkout and reset change some parts about the index and working directory as well.
Branches are nothing but entries in `.git/refs/heads`.

HEAD is the pointer to the current branch reference, which is in turn a pointer to the last commit made on that branch. 
That means HEAD will be the parent of the next commit that is created. The detached HEAD occurs, when
HEAD points directly to a commit instead of a branch. Nice for experimenting and by `git switch <branch>` one can go 
restore the pointer to a branch, if one would like to persist their changes in detached head mode, one needs to create a new branch
e.g. `git switch -c <branch-name>`.

Tags
Tags are either annotated or lightweight. 
A lightweight tag is a reference that never moves. 
An annotated tag is more complex, however. If you create an annotated tag, Git creates a tag object and then writes a reference to point to it rather than directly to the commit. 
You can see this by creating an annotated tag (using the -a option):
```
$ git tag -a v1.1 1a410efbd13591db07496601ebc7a059dd55cfe9 -m 'Test tag' 
$ cat .git/refs/tags/v1.1
9585191f37f7b0fb9444f35a9bf50de191beadc2

$ git cat-file -p 9585191f37f7b0fb9444f35a9bf50de191beadc2
object 1a410efbd13591db07496601ebc7a059dd55cfe9
type commit
tag v1.1
tagger Scott Chacon <schacon@gmail.com> Sat May 23 16:48:58 2009 -0700

Test tag
```
Notice that the object entry points to the commit SHA-1 value that you tagged. 
Also notice that it doesn’t need to point to a commit; you can tag any Git object.

Remotes
If you add a remote and push to it, Git stores the value you last pushed to that remote for each branch in the `.git/refs/remotes` directory.
Remote references differ from branches mainly in that they’re considered read-only. You can `git checkout` one, but  Git won’t symbolically reference HEAD to one,
so `git commit` never updates them directly.



Undoing/updating existing things in Git

Only locally commited changes
```
$ git commit --amend
```
This command takes your staging area and uses it for the commit. 


Unstaging/Unmodifying a Staged File
Unstaging
```
git reset HEAD <file>
```
or modern version
```
git restore --staged <file>
```
Unmodifying
```
git checkout -- CONTRIBUTING.md
```
or modern version
```
git restore <file>
```
Undoing commits
```
git revert <commit-or-ref>
```
Undoing merge commits
```
git revert -m <stage-whose-changes-should-be-kept> <commit-or-ref-from-which-to-start>
```
For the `<stage-whose-changes-should be kept>` part, one typically has `1` for the target (ours)
and `2` for the merge (theirs) branch
