How git reset works under the hood
`git reset` uses 3 steps by default (which can be modified via cli arguments)
1. `git reset --soft <commit-hash>`, move head and the pointer of the current branch to given commit (do not touch index or working dir)
(this is essentially the underlying mechanism of `git commit --ammend`)
2. `git reset --mixed <commit-hash>` Unstages (removes from index) all changes
3. `git reset hard <commit-hash>` 
One can apply those changes partially by passing instead a file name (which leverages the underlying patch-ids), 
this does not change the head and branch, but instead overwrites the local file with the version referenced and adds it to the index.


Git revert allows to undo commits, in case of multiple commits (which were not introduced by a merge commit)
usually one should start with the latest one in time and successively revert the ones before (reverse in time).

If one wants to apply specific commits from another branch (or some reverted ones), one can use `git cherry-pick <commit_1> <commit_2> ...`
In case that these are reverted ones (that is the one that is referenced by a revert commit (not the revert commit itself, as this is not recommended), 
it is most sensible to start with the historical first commit (so we work forward in time) and then the ones afterwards. 

Some operations for branches
Create branch
```
git branch <name-of-new-branch>
```
or create and switch
```
git checkout -b <name-of-new-branch> #or
git switch -c <name-of-new-branch>
```
Switch/Checkout branch
```
git checkout <name-of-branch> #or
git switch <name-of-branch>
```
Note that `git switch` is only intended for branches and is just a user-friendly version of `git checkout`
which also works for checking out specific files. 
Lists all branches with latest commits
```
git branch -v
```
Lists all remote branches
```
git ls-remote <remote>
git remote show <remote>
```
Delete branch
```
git branch -d <name-of-branch>
```
Applies only locally, to also delete the remote branch use
```
git push origin --delete <name-of-branch> 
```
Rename branch
```
$ git branch --move bad-branch-name corrected-branch-name
```
This change only applies locally, to apply those changes to a remote origin
```
git push --set-upstream origin corrected-branch-name
```

Git Rebase Finetuning
Suppose you wanted to include the changes from main on you feature branch (and not include this in the history as a merge), 
than the basic syntax is
```
git checkout <feature-branch>
git rebase main
# or multiple branches
git rebase --onto master <feature1> <feature2>
```
Something helpful to decide when to rebase and when to merge
***Do not rebase commits that exist outside your repository and that people may have based work on.***
(e.g. do not take the remote main branch and rewrite its history by rebasing it onto something else)
When you rebase stuff, you’re abandoning existing commits and creating new ones that are similar but different. 
If you push commits somewhere and others pull them down and base work on them, and then you rewrite those commits with git rebase and push them up again, 
your collaborators will have to re-merge their work and things will get messy when you try to pull their work back into yours.
Fixing this can be quite hard.


Git Merge Finetuning
Some vocabulary first, target branch refers to the branch one is currently on (ours).
The merge branch (theirs) is the branch whose commits are merged into the target branch.
Note that one can merge multiple branches (resolution strategy octopus) at once, which has some special cases not treated here.
The command for merging (if it is not implicitely started by something like `git pull`) is
```
git merge <feature-branch>
```

Git stores all of these versions in the index under “stages” which each have numbers associated with them. 
Stage 1 is the common ancestor, stage 2 is your version and stage 3 is from the MERGE_HEAD, the version you’re merging in (“theirs”).
To compare the changes after doing some resolutions that will be commited one can use the following:
```
git diff --ours # perspective from target branch before merge
git diff --theirs # perspective from merge branch before merge
git diff --base # perspective from latest common commit of the two branches 
```
The syntax of merging conflicts works like this
```
<<<<<<< HEAD:index.html
<div id="footer">contact : email.support@github.com</div>
=======
<div id="footer">
 please contact us at support@github.com
</div>
>>>>>>> featurebranch:index.html
```
The `<<< HEAD:file_name` refers to the state on the target branch while the part below `====`
refers to the changes from the merge branch. Git can also display this information together
with the base (latest common commit) state via the option `git checkout --conflict=diff3 <file-to-resolve>`.

To only accept changes from one stage/source one can use the following commands

```
git merge -Xours <merge-branch>
git merge -Xtheirs <merge-branch>
```
Or for specific files
```
git merge-file --ours <file-to-resolve>
git merge-file --ours <file-to-resolve>
```


Rewriting history
One can do quite advanced modifications of the git history with the help of 
```
git rebase -i
```
the interactive rebase mode. Of course this requires a force push to affect origins and should be done with care.

Some examples of what is possible are
- changing multiple commit messages
- reordering commits
- splitting commits
- squashing (fusioning subsequent) commits
- deleting commits
- changing the content of a commit (uses `git replace` under the hood)

How this is done in detail is easier to try out instead of explaining it.
Git rewinds to the latest commit not affected by the changes requested via `git rebase -i`
and then selectively reapplies the changes or stops at a commit, unstage its changes and let you introduce
multiple commits there (and remove files or changes that were committed or add them). This effectively rebases
this edited history on the latest unaffected commit. Many of them have the effect of changing all subsequent commits (e.g. deleting one).

Changing the content of a commit makes often sense in combination with the filter-branch functionality 
```
git filter-branch --tree-filter 'rm -f passwords.txt' HEAD
```
E.g. a secret was committed.
The --tree-filter option runs the specified command after each checkout of the project and then recommits the results. 
In this case, you remove a file called passwords.txt from every snapshot, whether it exists or not. 


Some notes on remotes and their branches
Adding a new remote
```
git remote add <shortname> <url>:
```
Renaming remotes
```
git remote rename <original-name> <new-name>
```
Removing remotes
```
git remote remove <remote-name>
```
Pushing to remotes
```
git push <remote> <branch>
```

Checking out a local branch from a remote-tracking branch automatically creates what is called a “tracking branch” 
(and the branch it tracks is called an “upstream branch”). 
Tracking branches are local branches that have a direct relationship to a remote branch. 
If you’re on a tracking branch and type git pull, Git automatically knows which server to fetch from and which branch to merge in.

When you clone a repository, it generally automatically creates a master branch that tracks origin/master. 
However, you can set up other tracking branches if you wish — ones that track branches on other remotes, or don’t track the master branch. 
The simple case is the example you just saw, running `git checkout -b <branch> <remote>/<branch>`. 
This is a common enough operation that Git provides the --track shorthand:
```
$ git checkout --track origin/<rbranch>
```
If the branch name you’re trying to checkout (a) doesn’t exist and (b) exactly matches a name on only one remote
than one does not even need to specify the remote and track info
```
$ git checkout <rbranch>
```
If you already have a local branch and want to set it to a remote branch you just pulled down, or want to change the upstream branch you’re tracking, you can use the -u or --set-upstream-to option to git branch to explicitly set it at any time.
```
$ git branch -u origin/serverfix
```
Recall that remote branches are also a form of references, remotes are stored as refspecs in the `.git/config` file, e.g.
```
[remote "origin"]
	url = https://github.com/schacon/simplegit-progit
	fetch = +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*
```
The format of the refspec is, first, an optional +, followed by <src>:<dst>, where <src> is the pattern for references on the remote side 
and <dst> is where those references will be tracked locally. The + tells Git to update the reference even if it isn’t a fast-forward

